I'm an ER nurse and I treated a patient  who thought he could fly and this is  that story I was working in one of the  busiest emergency rooms in downtown LA  running around treating my patients when  we had a young boy rushed in from a  nearby University and he was admitted  for a traumatic brain injury he finished  his first year of medical school and he  was throwing a party the first year of  medical school is definitely one of the  hardest so it was just natural for him  to celebrate his achievement  he drank alcohol and he took two 100  milligram pills of MDMA just also known  as ecstasy mixing the alcohol with the  ecstasy can heighten the effect of the  ecstasy and also increase the time of  effect in the ER we do see a lot of  patients abuse ecstasy and other  Hallucigenia I think a lot of nurses and  doctors because they see the effects of  it they don't try it  they don't go near it ecstasy can  sometimes make you believe that you see  or hear things that are not actually  there and he believed that he could fly  so without hesitation he jumped off a 74  balcony his friends thought he was  joking initially when they found out he  was being serious and he had actually  taken max to see an alcohol together it  was too late to stop him from falling  luckily he broke his fall by hitting a  tree while he was coming down if that  tree wasn't there I mean he could have  instantly died the tree reduced the  force of which his head hit the concrete  he was unconscious and he had obvious  signs of traumatic brain injury  irregular breathing there was clear  fluid that comes out of the nose and  ears which is cerebrospinal fluid also  he had a lot of pressure built up in his  brain it can cause permanent brain  damage if it's not treated promptly this  one cause seizure strokes and even  sometimes death in the ER everyone has  to act really fast especially when we  get a trauma case everyone has to be on  top of him maybe several nurses come to  one patient  and putting in IVs one nurses putting in  an IV one nurses securing the airway  making sure the patient's breathing  everything happens pretty fast and they  are almost just like you see in the  Hollywood movies the family of our  patient is also our patient obviously  this was a younger patient so his  parents were traumatized especially his  mother who was initially had heard what  happened and thankfully they were in LA  at the time and she came in and she was  just crying hysterically and I was the  first person who saw her and just I held  her hand and told her that you know we  were taking care of the situation we  were doing as much as we can all the  nurses and doctors were doing as much as  they can to stabilize her son her only  Son and hopefully save his life  well usually when an coma is induced  it's because the brain is working so  hard to take care of the trauma so when  that happens the brain starts to swell  up and it's pushing on the skull and it  can cause permanent damage so in order  to stop the brain from working so much a  doctor will go in and use medication to  induce coma  sometimes when patients come off a coma  not only are they affected by the  initial trauma to the brain but they're  affected by the coma and the medications  that they were given some patients get  out of a coma and there they stay in a  vegetative state or v/s is what we call  it or sometimes in a minimally conscious  state where they can respond to stimuli  but they're not doing anything else so  the patient was in induced coma for two  weeks  finally and thankfully he his pressure  in the brain decreased and his vital  signs returned to normal so to my  surprise when the patient awoke he  didn't have any changes in his  personality his mood he didn't lose any  memory he was very lucky so all the  doctors and nurses worked really hard to  get him to where he was at the end of  his day he did come back several months  later to thank all the nurses that we're  still working there all the doctors  which was nice he brought a box of  doughnuts he was young and he had a  near-death experience which I'm sure  changed his life forever and the way he  looks at life and the way he treats his  patient because he's gonna be a doctor  one day especially as a trauma nurse I  do take pride in taking care of every  patient even if they just come in for  having a bad trip on ecstasy  you know just even if it's a  psychological help because we do do that  a lot in the ER as psychologically  helped patients get better it's still  taking me time to kind of shut off my  brain after I go home from the ER or the  ICU and not think about the things that  I saw that day sometimes just going home  and talking to my sister about it  it was also a health care professional  talking to a friend or anything and just  kind of letting it go and you just let  it go hard  [Music]
 

